\section{Size}{

By default the scene is laid out again at the size of the location
(\code{resize = TRUE}), exactly as \code{\link[=render]{render()}} would at that size, so text keeps
its point size and nothing is stretched. \code{resize = FALSE} keeps the scene's
own size, anchored at the location's top-left corner.
}

\section{Text and fonts}{

Each label names the font vellum measured it with. PowerPoint uses that font
when the machine opening the deck has it and substitutes another when it does
not; the box is anchored so a wider substitute grows away from the label's
anchor (right-aligned tick labels stay right-aligned) rather than drifting.
Fonts are not embedded. \code{text = "outline"} writes glyph outlines instead:
identical everywhere, but no longer editable as text. \code{md()} and \code{tex()}
labels, halos and text set on a path are always outlines, as in SVG.
}

\section{What cannot be expressed}{

DrawingML has no clipping, blend modes, masks or group opacity. Clipped
geometry is cut in advance: fills to the visible area, strokes along their
centreline (a round or square cap at a cut end reaches half a line width past
the edge), pictures cropped. Native text cannot be cut: a label crossing a
clip edge is drawn whole, with a warning (outlined text is cut exactly).
Blend modes, masks and group opacity are not yet applied, and a warning says
so. Rasters become pictures.

For charts whose \emph{data} stays editable in PowerPoint, see the mschart
package; this writes the drawing, not the data.
}

